{{also|dispensé}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|dispenser}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|dispensare||to weigh out, pay out, distribute, regulate, manage, control, dispense}}, frequentative of {{m|la|dispendere||to weigh out}}, from {{m|la|dis-||apart}} + {{m|la|pendere||to weigh}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈpɛns/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-dispense.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛns}}
* {{hyphenation|en|dis|pense}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|dispens}}

# To [[issue]], [[distribute]], or give out.
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=He is delighted to '''dispense''' a share of it to all the company.}}
#* '''1955''', William Golding, ''The Inheritors'', Faber and Faber 2005, p.40:
#*: The smoky spray seemed to trap whatever light there was and to '''dispense''' it subtly.
# To apply, as laws to particular cases; to administer; to execute; to manage; to direct.
#: ''to '''dispense''' justice''
#* '''1662''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''To the Lord Chancellor Hyde''
#*: While you '''dispense''' the laws, and guide the state.
# To [[supply]] or [[make up]] a medicine or prescription.
#: ''The pharmacist '''dispensed''' my tablets.''
#: ''An optician can '''dispense''' spectacles.''
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To give a [[dispensation]] to (someone); to [[excuse]].
#* {{RQ:Florio Montaigne Essayes|chapter=34|book=II|passage=After his victories, he often gave them the reines to all licenciousnesse, for a while '''dispencing''' them from all rules of military discipline{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History|11|It was resolved that all members of the House who held commissions, should be '''dispensed''' from parliamentary attendance.}}
#* {{RQ:Johnson Savage}}
#*: He appeared to think himself born to be supported by others, and '''dispensed''' from all necessity of providing for himself.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[compensate]]; to make up; to make amends.
#* {{RQ:Spenser FQ|1|3|stanza=30}}
#*: One loving howre / For many yeares of sorrow can '''dispence'''
#* {{rfdatek|en|Gower}}
#*: His sin was '''dispensed''' / With gold, whereof it was compensed.

